{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790342205,"nonce":"+If/wyJK62O1fmJ4VabATEJjSTY=","title":"HIR costs 700 to 900 bytes per instruction","message":"A HirValue is 240 B (HirConstant 96 inline, a per-value HashSet of uses of 48 B plus a heap table, HirType 56), a HirInstruction 192 B, a HirBlock 208 B, and every constant is its own value. Even with exact capacity, values cost 445 B each.\n\nEvidence. NUC instrumented, api lowered module: 191,511 values = 101.3 MB, of which 67,020 are constants and 86,677 have no uses; blocks 41.8 MB (instruction vecs 33.7). About 45 % of the uses sets are empty. sizeof: HirValue 240, HirInstruction 192, HirBlock 208, HirConstant 96, HirType 56, HirPhi 88, interpreter Op 16.\n\nSavings: roughly halves every HIR figure (module, scratch, interp and optimised bodies): api 60 to 70 MB, nextvar 25 to 50, locals 150 or more; also the library body decode at start-up.\n\nFix: intern constants per function; move the uses set out of HirValue and rebuild def-use on demand; box the rare large HirConstant and HirType variants. Target under 100 B per value. Touches every pass that reads value.uses; needs HIR identity on the kernels and ZYNTAX_CHECK_HIR_USES=1. Package MEM-hir-compact, after MEM-hir-capacity and the in-flight SSA work.","files":null}]}